<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kūkaniloko</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kukaniloko Birthstones</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kūkaniloko is a sacred site near Wahiawā in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, where chiefly women came to give birth so that their children would be of the highest rank. About 180 basalt stones mark the site, some carved with petroglyphs. Tradition says that for centuries the high chiefs of Oʻahu, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakuhihewa">Kākuhihewa</a>, were born there. It is now a protected historic site.</p></div>
